From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] luma: tiled windows follow the work area when the Dash moves

When the Dash frees an edge or takes one (Shell .119: an empty edge
reserves nothing), Mutter changes the work area and the tiling manager's
layout follows, but the windows already in tiles kept their old size: the
freed space stayed empty until each window was tiled again. A window's tile
is stored relative to the work area it was made in, so on a work-area
change every tiled window on that monitor is eased into the same tile of the
new work area, with the layout's gaps. Floating windows are not touched,
minimized ones wait, and maximized ones are re-fitted by Mutter itself.

Only once the layout has settled, so a resume or a monitor hotplug does
not make windows flicker between places: the re-tile waits until the work
area has not changed for 500 ms and window memory has finished putting
windows back, moves each window at most once per settled work area, never
to another monitor, and stops moving a window that was moved 3 times in
2 s (logged for Vitals as reflow-stopped). Window memory's own put-back
after a monitor change waits the same 500 ms for the layout to settle,
puts each window back once per settled layout, and leaves alone (logged as
restore-churn) a window it moved 3 times in 2 s.

The settle is only for a resume or a monitor change. A change the Dash
makes itself (an island came or went; Shell .121 sets Main.shelf.
workAreaMotion) is followed at once, on the Dash's own curve and duration:
each window glides from where it was into its tile in one continuous move
or resize (Main.wm.lumaGlideFrame, no cross-fade), and is simply moved with
reduced motion. When a band arrives the Dash says where the work area is
going first (work-area-changing, Main.shelf.workAreaNext), so the tiles
move with the band before Mutter could push a window out of it; when one
goes, they follow as soon as Mutter has the larger area.

A window put in a tile stays exactly there: once the client has answered,
its frame is compared with the tile and put back (at most twice, logged as
tile-corrected or tile-refused). A client answering a resize late while
Mutter still had the old work area left a lower tile's window pushed up,
with a gap above the Dash, and the re-tile then skipped it.

One gap everywhere: the Dash's reservation on an edge already holds the
margin a window keeps from an island (what maximized windows use), so the
outer gap on that edge is the rest of the gap, and the whole gap on an edge
the Dash does not hold (lumaOuterGaps, Main.shelf.reservedMargins). A tiled
window is then one gap from another window, from an island and from the
screen, on every edge.

Once per user (luma-settings-migration): an outer gap of 0, set while the
Dash kept a margin on every edge, becomes the gap between windows, so
tiles keep a gap at edges the Dash leaves empty; and Tiling Shell's own
panel button, turned back on, goes back to its default (off). Tiling lives
in Quick Options.

A window that closes while it is held in its tile takes its hold with it
(the check is dropped on unmanaging), and only a window can be held at all:
anything else is refused and logged (tile-hold-type) instead of being
called as one, which is what raised "window.get_compositor_private is not
a function" from the check.

